During an interview on NBC's "Meet the Press", Araghchi responded to reports suggesting the US might resume bombing Iran after the midterm elections, saying, "We are now ready for a war." He emphasized that if attacked again, Iran would firmly respond, "Even if it means a war of annihilation."

Nevertheless, Araghchi also stated that Iran remains open to diplomatic solutions, "Now it is up to President Trump to make the choice."

Last week, Iran submitted a proposal to reopen the Strait of Hormuz as part of a peace agreement, requesting its reopening within seven days. The US President Donald Trump rejected the proposal on September 26, claiming Iran was desperate to reach an agreement because they had lost too much.

Araghchi clarified that Iran is still willing to negotiate, "We are not ready to give up on diplomacy, but we are trying to make peace, as we believe we should not miss any chance for peace."

He stressed that Iran is willing to negotiate and face any challenges, "Even if the conflict escalates to a war of annihilation, we will still resist aggression."

On September 26, the Iranian Revolutionary Guard claimed to have detained a US Navy unmanned underwater vehicle (UUV) in the Strait of Hormuz. The claim was made by Tasnim News Agency, which quoted the Revolutionary Guard, stating it was a Remus 600 type, which has been handed over to experts for data extraction. The US Navy did not immediately respond to the request for comment.

On September 25, Araghchi announced at the UN headquarters in New York that Iran had submitted a proposal to reopen the Strait of Hormuz through Qatar. If the US took necessary actions, the Strait could be reopened within seven days, restoring normal shipping. However, The Wall Street Journal reported on September 25 that Trump rejected Iran's proposal and told his aides he might resume bombing Iran after the US midterm elections in November.

Araghchi added that the mediation party had not yet conveyed the US's formal rejection to Tehran. He insisted that only negotiation could break the deadlock; as for reopening the Strait of Hormuz, Iran insisted that the US must first meet its conditions. Araghchi reiterated that as part of a peace agreement, Iran was willing to reopen the Strait. As a condition, Iran requested the release of frozen Iranian funds and assets, and the ability to sell oil.

US Ambassador to the UN, Michael Waltz, criticized Iran's latest proposal as a "fake attitude" that the US could not accept. He said Trump would retain all options.

Regarding the US's potential attacks on Iran, the Iranian military spokesman, Amirali Hajizadeh, stated on September 27 that the Iranian armed forces are fully prepared to fight.
